  "transcript": "# Space resilience\n\n## What happens when satellites fail?\n> Satellites help armed forces see, communicate and navigate, but access to those services cannot be taken for granted.\n\nHelen Maguire: Which dependence on space-based equipment worries you most for our armed forces?\nDr Michelle Howard: Reliable access to communications and satellite images worries me. Russia disrupted Ukraine’s satellite internet at the start of its invasion, affecting military command. In March 2025, the US suspended Ukraine’s access to satellite imagery used to track Russian troops.\nDr Michelle Howard: I’m also concerned about relying on a company’s service. In October, SpaceX imposed speed restrictions on Starshield, which Ukraine used for military communications. I believe that badly affected its ability to communicate with drones.\nMajor General Paul Tedman: Without space services, forces lose much of their ability to see what’s happening, find their position, communicate and fight. We usually assume those services will be there.\nNathan Mathiot: The UK also needs a reliable way to launch replacement satellites and restore services if they’re lost. That’s a particular concern for the future.\nLord Sedwill: If Britain lost access to its own or American space services, how well could its forces fight? And won’t plans for more automated equipment make that dependence greater?\nMajor General Paul Tedman: They could still fight with maps and compasses, but not nearly as efficiently or effectively. Around 3,000 systems in an Army brigade rely on space. And yes, greater use of automated equipment increases the dependence.\nMajor General Paul Tedman: We need to ask two things: can we discourage an attack on those services, and, if one happens, can we absorb it while still giving our forces what they need? I can’t confidently answer yes.\nMajor General Paul Tedman: The defence plans set out what’s wanted and how to fund it. My worry is whether we can deliver it quickly enough to keep up with the growing threat.\n\n\n## Depending on allies without losing control\n> Witnesses said Britain cannot quickly replace allied systems, but should secure greater control over the services and equipment it needs.\n\nDr Michelle Howard: The planned armed forces will share more information and use more automated systems, all of which makes them more dependent on space. Losing space services wouldn’t stop them overnight, but it would gradually reduce how far, fast and accurately they could operate.\nLord Sedwill: So does dependence on space put the whole plan to modernise the armed forces at risk if those services fail?\nDr Michelle Howard: I wouldn’t quite put it that way, but achieving the effectiveness promised by that plan relies heavily on space—perhaps more heavily than people realise.\nDr Michelle Howard: Britain can’t make every component or service itself, and it will rely heavily on allies, especially the US, for years. The practical question is how to keep secure, consistent access. For example, Britain could retain the ability to approve software changes to its equipment without seeking permission abroad, and maintain local manufacturing so it can replace equipment after an attack.\nLord Sedwill: Are we also dependent on technology from countries outside our close group of allies?\nDr Michelle Howard: This isn’t my area of expertise, but chips and rare earth minerals used in important systems do come from non-allies. We need more work tracing suppliers before we can understand the full risk.\nNathan Mathiot: Government and industry need a longer-term relationship. Rather than mainly giving grants for research, government should gradually buy working equipment and services over time. That would give companies a steadier customer and help keep the UK industry strong.\nNathan Mathiot: Different needs call for different choices: Britain may need to control some services itself, secure dependable access to others, and buy others from trusted partners or the commercial market. Working with allies such as Japan could stretch a limited budget, though it creates dependencies too.\nLord Sedwill: Can technology solve problems such as fake or blocked GPS signals, or will those attacks remain possible however much we spend?\nNathan Mathiot: I’m not the technical expert to settle that. I don’t think there’s one fix: options being explored include ground-based alternatives to satellite navigation and using different satellite providers. Having several ways to provide a service could make an attacker less confident of knocking it out.\n\n\n## Threats that are no longer theoretical\n> Witnesses described several ways satellites can be disrupted or attacked, and said space’s importance is still not widely understood.\n\nLord Godson: What threats do Russia, China and others pose in space? And do the public and government understand those threats well enough?\nMajor General Paul Tedman: The threats range from temporarily blocking signals to grabbing satellites or firing missiles at them. Russia has stated an intention to put a nuclear weapon in orbit. Most, if not all, of these types of capability have been demonstrated; they aren’t science fiction.\nMajor General Paul Tedman: In my view, China has more sophisticated space capabilities but is more predictable in its decisions. Russia has less capability but has acted in ways we didn’t expect. Together, they’re a serious problem; their equipment for disrupting or attacking space services has grown considerably since 2021.\nMajor General Paul Tedman: Space specialists can argue for more attention, but it carries more weight when senior military leaders explain why they need space services. Defence has added space and cyber operations to its responsibilities without a corresponding rise in the overall budget.\nDr Michelle Howard: There’s a long way to go with the public too. In a recent UK survey, 21% of people first associated space with aliens, while only 8% thought of communications and connectivity. Rockets and astronauts attract interest; explaining the less exciting services we use every day is harder.\n\n\n## What government should buy—and from whom\n> The witnesses wanted clearer government purchasing plans, but named different priorities and disagreed over buying only from allies.\n\nLord Watts: What should government do to help defence space companies, and which services should it spend public money on first?\nNathan Mathiot: Companies need to see how much government expects to spend each year and what it plans to buy. That would give them confidence to invest in the UK. My priority is the ability to launch satellites: the UK and Europe depend too much on existing launch capacity, including SpaceX.\nDr Michelle Howard: Government should be clear about what it wants and publish a dependable schedule of contracts. I wouldn’t prescribe every spending priority, but ISAM—servicing and manufacturing equipment in orbit—is an area where the UK has a real strength.\nMajor General Paul Tedman: Government also needs to make clear who’s in charge of space policy and speed up how it buys equipment. For capability, I’d prioritise space control: the plans already identify it as an area where Britain isn’t investing enough.\nLord Watts: Should Britain buy national security equipment only from allies?\nMajor General Paul Tedman: No. We should diversify our suppliers without cutting ourselves off from our closest allies in space.\nDr Michelle Howard: Yes. For national security equipment, we should buy only from allies.\nNathan Mathiot: I agree with Michelle on buying from allies.\n",
